# PINS - the upstream decoders of RayClay's media profile (TOML).
#
# The upstream files beside this one (pristine but for the twelve patches RAYCLAY-PATCHES.txt lists in dr_flac.h and
# stb_vorbis.c), their license texts (LICENSES/, and libwebp's own COPYING, PATENTS and
# AUTHORS at the root of libwebp/, where its sources' headers name them) and this file are DATA ONLY. RayClay's own
# profile code is first-party: the audio bridge (rc_media_profile.c, rc_media_profile.h and the profile's
# CMakeLists.txt) and the WebP bridge (rc_image_webp_profile.c, its header
# and rc_image_webp_names.h); all six ship beside this file, in the same
# mediaprofile/ directory.
#
# Read by:
#   - a checksum check: each [[pin]]'s `file`, and each of its [[pin.files]], must hash to its `sha256` and
#     measure `size` bytes; README.md's list of every shipped file's sha256 must say the same;
#   - the profile's identity TAG, a hash of its pins and its sources taken whenever they change: the anchor lines
#     (RCI_MEDIA_PROFILE_ANCHOR in rc_media_profile.h and rayclay.h, RCI_IMAGE_WEBP_ANCHOR in
#     rc_image_webp_profile.h and rayclay.h) name it, so a profile change renames both anchors: every program
#     that calls rcMediaOpen on Linux, or rcLoadImage off Apple, is then rebuilt against the new archive, never
#     linked to a stale one. A build recomputes nothing: the TAG is the string the profile shipped with.
#
# Imported 2026-11-04. Each file was checked on import against two independent sources: its raw download at the
# pinned commit (sha256 below), and the git blob id GitHub's contents API answers for the same path at the same commit
# (`blob` below; `git hash-object <file>` recomputes it). For a pin with `patches` (dr_flac.h, stb_vorbis.c, patched
# 2026-11-05), the download is `upstream_size` / `upstream_sha256` and `blob` is upstream's file's, while `size` and
# `sha256` are the shipped, patched file's. stb_vorbis.c also matched byte for byte a second,
# independent copy of nothings/stb's repository. libwebp: the release tarball's OpenPGP signature verified
# (the WebP release signing key, `signer` below), and each of the 87 files shipped matched byte for byte the same
# path in GitHub's archive of the release tag's commit. nestegg and libopus were imported 2026-11-07; the comment
# above each one's [[pin]] says how its files were checked.
#
# Why these: the profile first named dr_mp3 0.7.3 and dr_flac master f1b5fc367fc6 (after its 2026-10-28 seek fixes, "Fix a
# possible crash with seeking"); on 2026-10-30 upstream released both as tags, dr_mp3 0.7.4 and dr_flac 0.13.4, which
# carry those fixes, so the profile pins the released tags. stb_vorbis 1.22 is the latest release of nothings/stb's
# decoder (its last commit to the file). Each is royalty-free: MP3's licensing program ended 2017-04-23,
# FLAC and Vorbis were never patent-encumbered. libwebp 1.6.0 is the latest release; BSD-3-Clause
# with Google's royalty-free patent grant (PATENTS). Its decoder and demux only, the files upstream's own CMake and
# Makefile.am build libwebpdecoder and libwebpdemux from, without the MIPS32, MIPS DSP R2 and MSA sources (no
# RayClay target is MIPS: an x86, Arm or wasm build compiles them to nothing) and without the encoder, sharpyuv,
# mux, examples and build scripts. nestegg (ISC; Firefox's WebM demuxer) and libopus 1.6.1 (the latest release of
# the reference codec; BSD-3-Clause with royalty-free patent licenses) are WebM's demuxer and its Opus decoder for
# Linux's video player (added 2026-11-07), pristine, decoders only.

