# libvpx's VP8 and VP9 decoders, for Linux's player: 1.17.0 is the latest release, BSD-3-Clause with
# Google's royalty-free patent grant (PATENTS). `dir` is its root, each [[pin.files]] one file shipped there, from
# the release tag `tag` (the annotated tag object `tag_object`) at `commit`, cloned from `url`, the project's own
# repository. Each of the 177 upstream files matched byte for byte the same path in a second clone of the tag from
# `mirror`, GitHub's mirror. The six marked `generated` are not upstream's files: upstream's configure wrote them
# from the same tree with the options in `configure` (Fedora 44, gcc 16.2.1), a portable C build that runs on any
# CPU with no assembler (vpx_config.h, the run-time dispatch tables vp8_rtcd.h, vp9_rtcd.h, vpx_dsp_rtcd.h and
# vpx_scale_rtcd.h, all naming the C functions, and vpx_version.h); the profile builds from them and configures
# nothing. The files are the 71 .c files of the 82 that build compiles into libvpx.a which the decoder interface
# reaches (vp8dx.h's two decoders, their objects' references followed), and the 103 upstream headers they include;
# the other 11 (the encoder API, the image allocator, the frame scalers, a YUV writer, the build string of
# vpx_config.c, and helpers no decoder object names) are left out. RayClay's rc_mp_vpx_names.h, beside the bridge
# (rc_media_profile.c), renames every global those objects define into rci_media_vpx_lib_
# (written from the symbols those objects define); the TAG covers it.
